#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551416} :activity/id #uuid "905428db-8bf9-5611-a5f5-3a24c22143f5" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048933 :topic/id #uuid "cd37bb30-463c-55ad-8ec5-08aa1d921272" :topic/math-academy-id 4238} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551364 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389636 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f0a6205e-2924-5cbb-a37c-53602e79f359" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389638} {:answer/id #uuid "3fe0747c-a561-57f7-8630-821f8a80e665" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389639} {:answer/id #uuid "b8eed71d-054b-5407-9d91-8e57f3cc52dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389640} {:answer/id #uuid "21a52435-a11d-5b4c-bfb8-496323584d9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389641} {:answer/id #uuid "8a78e66d-9ed7-5211-b359-0bca69ed1942" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389642}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389638} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ab1c9da-4432-585e-811e-f80370a2d619" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389637}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2f869ed0-df60-5562-b5c5-b1ddb2293f45" :question/math-academy-id "q-170504" :question/problem "The maximum value of the quadratic form $Q(x)=2{x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}-{x}_{3}^{2}-2{x}_{2}{x}_{3},$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to $2.$ Which of the following is a vector $x$ at which the maximum is attained?" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-2I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{max}}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0e8b4a6b-0e73-59e4-8a2d-579a90df12b3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551460}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551391 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389855 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f929e6fc-859c-554c-b8d1-7edc7d26c0b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389857} {:answer/id #uuid "c5ef524e-3272-5724-9dcf-18dac71f64f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389858} {:answer/id #uuid "5908cb4e-dd22-5985-bab6-7726588673da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389859} {:answer/id #uuid "da4cfa35-5f24-5531-a00c-981216fded81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389860} {:answer/id #uuid "632432f1-e342-5b99-b90c-6df4e61f0365" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389861}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389861} :answer-field/id #uuid "4acab487-d1e8-587d-9ab8-b10c8019f1b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389856}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "9517d41a-5a17-54a5-b8d2-6ea1cb7908a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-179407" :question/problem "The maximum value of $Q(x)=-{x}_{1}^{2}+8{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+8{x}_{2}^{2},$ when ${x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2}=4,$ is $12.$ Find a vector $x$ at which the maximum is attained." :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n Notice that we can write the constraint equation as follows:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\n This is an ellipse with semiaxes $a=2$ (along the ${x}_{1}$-axis) and $b=1$ (along the ${x}_{2}$-axis). If we introduce new variables\n\n$\\,$ ${y}_{1}=\\frac{{x}_{1}}{2}\\,$ and $\\,{y}_{2}={x}_{2},$\n\n the equation of the ellipse will transform into ${y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}=1$ or $‖y‖=1.$\n\n So, introducing a change-of-coordinate matrix \n\n \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n \n\n we obtain that $x=Py.$ Substituting this into the expression of the quadratic form, we get\n\n \n\n$$\nQ(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax=(Py)^{T}A\\,(Py)={y}^{T}\\,({P}^{T}AP)y={Q}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\n As a result, since ${P}^{T}=P,$ the matrix of our quadratic form in new coordinates is \n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & ={P}^{T}\\,AP \\\\  & =PAP \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 8 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n Now, we maximize ${Q}^{′}(y)$ when $||y||=1,$ as usual.\n\n Since we are told that the maximum value of the quadratic form is $12,$ we have\n\n \n\n$$\nmax{{Q}^{′}(y)\\,|\\,‖y‖=1}={\\lambda }_{max}=12.\n$$\n\n Next, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}=12:$\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-12I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 8\\end{bmatrix}-12\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-16 & 8 \\\\ 8 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Seeking a non-zero solution of $(B-12I)y=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\n Finally, we map this vector back to the initial coordinate  system using the change-of-coordinates matrix:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{max}} & =P{u}_{\\text{max}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3dc6c1fd-2f16-537e-9ca0-101cbae17c52"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551416 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514484 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d3a7fdb-eefe-51a3-9ec9-f7c801ef2416" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513564} {:answer/id #uuid "7200c83a-2142-5d00-85db-a56671970e08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513836} {:answer/id #uuid "7223184f-b1ee-5d86-a4ef-23d7fc3ee742" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513837} {:answer/id #uuid "924f6560-699b-5491-8855-7e4d83c4748a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513912} {:answer/id #uuid "ab6ff50e-3a08-5efc-b570-80d38bf9dae9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513979}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513837} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd3f9993-5662-5d10-ad04-17586e5bdd3b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514353}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fd31a01e-94fe-56c0-b345-2a845f22f0d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-138131" :question/problem "The maximum  value of the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2},$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to $4.$ Which of the following is a vector $x$ at which the maximum  is attained?" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}=4:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-4I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "80a0a003-9f06-5d20-9a5f-8b2ed41c3e0e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551364}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551422 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410197 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27fa6c83-fea5-559c-b3af-e6a7e1618346" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410169} {:answer/id #uuid "3bb153c6-9455-5e09-b9d4-e4c936682c6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410172} {:answer/id #uuid "566bcdea-96b1-557a-bdcd-81a53378a8b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410173} {:answer/id #uuid "a93b100e-c392-5adc-a3ed-bee963bcaac6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410185} {:answer/id #uuid "b119d151-632a-5e08-8a6e-94f8c7ba763c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410186}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410169} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5b0d1a4-b3d8-5bd2-80e4-83dea5aa94c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410194}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "dea55e7b-1cc0-500b-8640-ae39dddce75c" :question/math-academy-id "q-170560" :question/problem "The maximum value of $Q(x)=4{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2},$ when $9{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2}=36,$ is $25.$ Which of the following is a vector $x$ at which the maximum is attained?" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNotice that we can write the constraint equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse with semiaxes $a=2$ (along the ${x}_{1}$-axis) and $b=3$ (along the ${x}_{2}$-axis). If we introduce new variables\n\n$\\,$ ${y}_{1}=\\frac{{x}_{1}}{2}\\,$ and $\\,{y}_{2}=\\frac{{x}_{2}}{3},$\n\nthe equation of the ellipse will transform into ${y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}=1$ or $‖y‖=1.$\n\nSo, introducing a change-of-coordinate matrix \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain that $x=Py.$ Substituting this into the expression of the quadratic form, we get\n\n$$\nQ(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax=(Py)^{T}A\\,(Py)={y}^{T}\\,({P}^{T}AP)y={Q}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nAs a result, since ${P}^{T}=P,$ the matrix of our quadratic form in new coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & ={P}^{T}\\,AP \\\\  & =PAP \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}16 & 12 \\\\ 12 & 9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we maximize ${Q}^{′}(y)$ when $‖y‖=1,$ as usual.\n\nSince we are told that the maximum value of the quadratic form is $25,$ we have\n\n$$\nmax\\{{Q}^{′}(y)\\,|\\,‖y‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{max}=25.\n$$\n\nNext, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}=25:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-25I & =\\begin{bmatrix}16 & 12 \\\\ 12 & 9\\end{bmatrix}-25\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 12 \\\\ 12 & -16\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(B-25I)y=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=5,$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we map this vector back to the initial coordinate  system using the change-of-coordinates matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{max}} & =P{u}_{\\text{max}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8a01626c-0fe4-5c01-be55-c9b98f53762d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551391}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551460 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284641 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01f3ea30-3f9d-5508-ab40-ee2dba4f0888" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "70" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281708} {:answer/id #uuid "2a5452df-294d-5701-b4af-bbf16d6e0530" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282056} {:answer/id #uuid "4e6d984c-3905-5e24-bf07-c5dd11a46a31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282364} {:answer/id #uuid "bb2908d2-0e8d-5999-a64a-a03cd4b68ae9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "90" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283306} {:answer/id #uuid "c1eb5798-946a-56c8-9937-6c6a91e2207e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "80" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283374}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283306} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3ab9ad5-298c-5e24-9d44-dc14f057d818" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284336}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "80991c77-0c00-52ac-bae2-41a3b83ff730" :question/math-academy-id "q-170515" :question/problem "Find the maximum value of $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}-18{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+81{x}_{2}^{2}$ given that ${x}_{1}^{2}+9{x}_{2}^{2}=9.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -9 \\\\ -9 & 81\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNotice that we can write the constraint equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse with semiaxes $a=3$ (along the ${x}_{1}$-axis) and $b=1$ (along the ${x}_{2}$-axis). If we introduce new variables\n\n$\\,$ ${y}_{1}=\\frac{{x}_{1}}{3}\\,$ and $\\,{y}_{2}={x}_{2},$ \n\nthe equation of the ellipse will transform into ${y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}=1$ or $‖y‖=1.$\n\nSo, introducing a change-of-coordinates matrix \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain that $x=Py.$ Substituting this into the expression of the quadratic form, we get\n\n$$\nQ(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax=(Py)^{T}A\\,(Py)={y}^{T}\\,({P}^{T}AP)y={Q}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since ${P}^{T}=P,$ the matrix of our quadratic form in new coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & ={P}^{T}\\,AP \\\\  & =PAP \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -9 \\\\ -9 & 81\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -27 \\\\ -9 & 81\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -27 \\\\ -27 & 81\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we maximize ${Q}^{′}(y)$ when $‖y‖=1,$ as usual. Calculating the eigenvalues $B,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|B-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}9-\\lambda  & -27 \\\\ -27 & 81-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (9-\\lambda )(81-\\lambda )-729 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-90\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -90) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =0,\\,90\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nmax\\{{Q}^{′}(y)\\,|\\,‖y‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}=90.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e6632206-5039-5291-b483-15e817232891" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551422}}] :activity/title "Constrained Optimization of Quadratic Forms: Determining Where Extrema are Attained" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550355}]}
